5. Her current daily schedule is described as crepuscular, active during dawn and dusk. (Verbatim: "my schedule is technically technically crepuscular at this point")
6. She has knowledge about owl eye color indicating activity patterns: orange for crepuscular, yellow for diurnal, black for nocturnal. (Verbatim: "orange eyes means they're crepuscular... yellow eyes they're usually day species... black eyes are for night active species")
7. She has experience identifying bird species and their traits, including understanding feather regrowth in parrots. (Verbatim: "they do grow back... but in some cases underlying skin structures are so damaged that the plucking feathers won't return")
8. She has encountered parrots with unusual behaviors, such as mimicking a smoker’s cough or using frequent swear words. (Verbatim: "would mimic a smoker's cough over and over again... every other word that came out of that parent's mouth was a swear word")
9. She has experience with animal rescue and rehabilitation efforts. (Verbatim: "yeah so they didn't like me... but i don't know like even though i went to always feed them")
10. She has observed ostriches displaying mating behavior toward humans, particularly female humans. (Verbatim: "every time the female would get ignored and anytime a female human would walk past hicken ostrich punk would come running up being like hello hello") [end of text]



1. She previously worked at a zoo, where she interacted with visitors and cared for animals. (Verbatim: "i didn't take care of the penguins i'm glad i didn't though because like the penguins stink... you will smell like fish forever")
2. She looked after ring-tailed lemurs and tufted lemurs during her time at the zoo. (Verbatim: "i looked after ring-tailed lemurs... and what was the other one i think the other one's tufted")
3. She encountered an Eurasian eagle owl that appeared to take a special interest in her, behaving unusually. (Verbatim: "there was a um was it eurasian eurasian eagle owl where i couldn't figure out why it was being so weird to me")
4. She had an ostrich attempt to court any woman walking by, including herself, with a mating dance. (Verbatim: "it would dance and i would see the female ostrich like behind him and i'd be like yo your partner's right behind you")
5. She mentions that the stork at the zoo had a habit of pecking butts. (Verbatim: "the stork just had a thing for for pecking butts and just aiming for the butt")
6. She expresses a desire to see a sun bear in the wild. (Verbatim: "i'd love to see one in the wild i don't know how aggressive they are compared to other bears")
7. She worked alongside a female colleague at the zoo who interpreted animal behavior, such as the owl liking her. (Verbatim: "the lady that i worked with she was like i uh yeah it likes you")
8. She clarifies that red pandas are not actually related to pandas and their classification is still debated. (Verbatim: "red pandas do not count no they're not actually related to really anything... they're still very confused what the heck a red panda is")
9. She notes that many people mistakenly believe penguins are fish, a common misconception she encountered at the zoo. (Verbatim: "a lot of people seem to think penguins are fish i don't know why but it was one of the very common misconceptions that would come up at the zoo")
10. She did not take care of penguins but heard they cause a persistent fishy odor on caretakers. (Verbatim: "i didn't take care of the penguins i'm glad i didn't though because like the penguins stink... you will be fishy forever") [end of text]



1. She lived in the jungle for a couple of months and participated in night walks. (Verbatim: "as you know i lived in the jungle for a couple of months and we did do night walks")
2. She is afraid of large spiders, especially those capable of eating frogs. (Verbatim: "what i was scared of most was more so the spiders um the spiders um the spiders used to scare me um the big big spiders like we're talking spiders that eat frogs kind of level")
3. She has encountered elephants in the wild and considers them the most dangerous creature she’s come across. (Verbatim: "i'd say the most the most dangerous thing i've encountered are elephants")
4. She believes elephants are fast, strong, and unstoppable, and advises keeping distance from them. (Verbatim: "the thing about elephants is people underestimate how fast they are um and and yeah how strong they are and that nothing literally stops them")
5. She prefers following elephant paths in the jungle over boar paths because they are wider and easier to traverse. (Verbatim: "i would much rather take an elephant path over a pig path... because the boar path is like much smaller and you have to get on your hands and knees and crawl")
6. She values environmental preservation and minimizes creating new paths in nature by using existing animal trails. (Verbatim: "you want to minimize the amount of paths that you're making because you want to be as non-destructive as possible")
7. She has been bitten by a hamster, and the bite bled for a long time. (Verbatim: "i've been bit by a hamster a hamster did go for the numb on my finger that did not stop bleeding for a long time")
8. She has never interacted much with guinea pigs and initially imagined them incorrectly. (Verbatim: "i've never really interacted with guinea pigs guinea pigs i mean this is exactly it i thought this is how guinea pigs kind of look in my brain")
9. She finds red squirrels very cute and wants to see one in the wild, but notes they are rare in England due to competition from gray squirrels. (Verbatim: "red squirrels are so cute i want to see a red squirrel in the wild but they're really hard to find in england because the gray squirrels came along and were like heck nah get out of here")
10. She owns a dog that reacts excitedly to squirrels, often attempting to jump and catch them despite never succeeding. (Verbatim: "my dog just taking turns and points and is like squirrel... she does the saddest little tiny jump") [end of text]



